es IV broad‑spectrum antibiotics and possible surgical drainage.\nRecognition of these differentials would shift management toward drainage procedures and may alter antibiotic choice.\n\n**Post‑airway disposition**: Admit to a pediatric intensive‑care unit. Monitor continuously for signs of re‑obstruction, maintain head‑up positioning, provide sedation as needed, and continue IV antibiotics for 7–10 days. Repeat lateral neck X‑ray or flexible laryngoscopy if clinical status worsens.\n\n**Positioning & handling**: Keep the child upright (tripod or semi‑upright), avoid supine positioning, limit handling, and do not perform a tongue‑depressor exam. Use calm, reassuring communication; have a parent present if it reduces distress.\n\n**Timing of antibiotics**: Give antibiotics **after** the airway is definitively secured (post‑intubation). Pre‑intubation antibiotics are not recommended because they can delay definitive airway control.\n\n**Follow‑up & discharge criteria**: Stable airway with no stridor at rest, afebrile for >24 h, able to tolerate oral intake, and completing the antibiotic course. Arrange ENT follow‑up within 48 h, primary‑care visit for vaccination updates, and educate caregivers on signs of recurrence.",
